WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.100
The first film showed ATP paying for glucose's uphill step.

2
00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:06.540
Now look at the wallet.

3
00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:11.440
Your body holds well under a kilogram of ATP at any moment.

4
00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:16.140
Yet you recycle roughly 40 kilograms of it every day.

5
00:00:17.420 --> 00:00:19.140
How can so small a pool keep up?

6
00:00:22.483 --> 00:00:25.463
It keeps up because ATP is not stockpiled.

7
00:00:26.523 --> 00:00:31.423
Each molecule is spent and remade hundreds of times a day, like cash passing through

8
00:00:31.423 --> 00:00:34.563
your hands. That raises two questions.

9
00:00:35.963 --> 00:00:37.343
Why is ATP good money?

10
00:00:38.563 --> 00:00:39.983
And what keeps remaking it?

11
00:00:40.917 --> 00:00:42.137
Start with the money.

12
00:00:43.717 --> 00:00:49.037
Rank phosphorylated compounds on a ladder by the free energy each releases, handing

13
00:00:49.037 --> 00:00:50.757
its phosphoryl group to water.

14
00:00:52.077 --> 00:00:54.997
Under standard conditions, transfers run downhill.

15
00:00:56.397 --> 00:00:57.837
ATP sits in the middle.

16
00:00:59.197 --> 00:01:01.887
Compounds above it can recharge ADP.

17
00:01:01.887 --> 00:01:05.937
ATP, in turn, phosphorylates compounds below it.

18
00:01:06.850 --> 00:01:10.170
That middle seat is what makes ATP good currency.

19
00:01:11.410 --> 00:01:14.750
It can be refilled from above, and it can pay for jobs below.

20
00:01:16.110 --> 00:01:17.530
Here is the idea to hold.

21
00:01:18.690 --> 00:01:23.910
ATP is cash, not savings: spent on the cell's work, and remade by burning fuel.

22
00:01:24.983 --> 00:01:31.523
Now the remaking. The cell refills ATP by oxidizing fuel, in three stages.

23
00:01:33.043 --> 00:01:38.583
Digestion breaks a steak, a potato and olive oil into amino acids, sugars and fatty

24
00:01:38.583 --> 00:01:44.563
acids. Most of their carbon then funnels into one molecule, acetyl-CoA.

25
00:01:45.643 --> 00:01:47.583
Different highways, one city center.

26
00:01:48.667 --> 00:01:53.607
The citric acid cycle oxidizes acetyl-CoA to carbon dioxide.

27
00:01:54.907 --> 00:02:00.327
The electrons it strips away load onto carriers, NADH and FADH₂.

28
00:02:01.767 --> 00:02:05.607
Handing those electrons on to oxygen drives ATP synthesis.

29
00:02:06.767 --> 00:02:10.587
So burning a fuel means stripping electrons from its carbons.

30
00:02:11.807 --> 00:02:18.047
Here is a test. Take one carbon from a fatty acid's long chain, and one carbon from

31
00:02:18.047 --> 00:02:21.727
glucose. Which one has more electrons to give?

32
00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:34.100
The fatty acid's. Its carbons are loaded with hydrogens, so they have many electrons

33
00:02:34.100 --> 00:02:40.280
to give. Every carbon in glucose is already bonded to oxygen, so it has fewer.

34
00:02:41.620 --> 00:02:47.700
That is the main reason fat yields about 9 kcal per gram, and carbohydrate about 4.

35
00:02:48.750 --> 00:02:50.610
So the body keeps two accounts.

36
00:02:52.050 --> 00:02:54.350
Fat is its main long-term store, the savings.

37
00:02:55.750 --> 00:02:59.790
ATP is the small pool of cash, emptied and refilled all day.

38
00:03:01.150 --> 00:03:04.330
Work keeps spending it, and burning fuel keeps paying it back.
